  "//browserslist": "WITHOUT THIS, NOTHING BLURRED IN PRODUCTION. Tailwind v4 optimises the stylesheet with Lightning CSS, and with no targets declared it prefixed against a default set that excludes Chrome - so every glass rule shipped as `-webkit-backdrop-filter` ALONE, with the standard property dropped. Chrome 151 does not support that alias (CSS.supports('-webkit-backdrop-filter','blur(2px)') === false), so on the built site the nav and all 14 cards measured `backdrop-filter: none` while the dev server measured blur(48px) and blur(8px). Every blur value tuned on 2026-10-15/16 was invisible on the deployment for this reason. Safari only gained the UNPREFIXED property in 18, so the correct output is BOTH: naming Chrome and Safari 16.4 here makes Lightning CSS emit the prefixed form for Safari 16-17 and the standard form for everything else. Do not remove without re-checking `backdrop-filter` in dist.",
  "browserslist": [
    "chrome >= 111",
    "edge >= 111",
    "firefox >= 128",
    "safari >= 16.4",
    "ios_saf >= 16.4"
  ],
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "//build": "`entry-graph` is the newest of these and the only one about SPEED rather than looks: it fails the build if a heavy package (Supabase, Zod, three, Vanta, xlsx, jsPDF, pptxgenjs) can be reached from `src/main.tsx` without crossing a dynamic `import()`. On 2026-10-16 the entry chunk was carrying the Supabase client and Zod — 70 KB gzipped on the public landing page — because of one top-level import in a leaf file six hops from the router, and the only trace in the build log was that `index-*.js` had grown. It is first in the chain because it reads sixteen files and answers in milliseconds, so it should fail before anything slow runs. See the header of scripts/entry-graph.mjs.",
